From: =?UTF-8?q?J=C3=A1nos=20Mik=C3=B3?=
 <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 23:20:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 015/154] atrium: default confirm-to-quit off so a Cmd+Q tap
 quits
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Cmd+Q resolved to IDC_EXIT (matched off the app menu's terminate: item),
which is a reserved command, so ChromeCommandDispatcherDelegate's
prePerformKeyEquivalent claimed the chord and ran chrome::AttemptUserExit
-> BrowserProcessPlatformPart::AttemptExit -> confirmQuitIfNeeded. With
kConfirmToQuitEnabled at Chromium's stock default of true, a plain tap
fails the hold-to-quit loop and the quit is cancelled - so Cmd+Q read as
"randomly does nothing" while the app-menu item still quit (that route
carries no key event, so the gate never engages).

Register the pref false instead. An earlier change shipped this same
default and validated it across a 4-state matrix; a later one reverted it to
stock, which re-broke the keystroke. The Settings > General and app-menu
toggles still turn the guard on, so the opt-in is intact.

Also correct the postPerformKeyEquivalent comment in the Atrium dispatcher
delegate: that Cmd+Q branch is unreachable, because prePerform claims the
chord first on every pass.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 chrome/browser/ui/cocoa/confirm_quit.cc | 27 ++++++++++++++-----------
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/cocoa/confirm_quit.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/cocoa/confirm_quit.cc
index c0c47804f79ec..a36a97c4bca77 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/cocoa/confirm_quit.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/cocoa/confirm_quit.cc
@@ -15,18 +15,21 @@ void RecordHistogram(ConfirmQuitMetric sample) {
 }
 
 void RegisterLocalState(PrefRegistrySimple* registry) {
-  // Atrium: registers the stock default (true - ask before
-  // quitting). A prior milestone shipped this default as
-  // false, because a plain Cmd+Q tap never satisfies the hold-to-quit loop
-  // and so silently no-ops in the empty/NTP and web-focused states. This
-  // supersedes that workaround: users demonstrably do hit this prompt
-  // (one report was of it rendering behind windows) and asked for an
-  // explicit way to turn it off, so the Settings > General "Ask before
-  // quitting" toggle (AtriumHostOps.set_confirm_to_quit) is now the
-  // supported opt-out, and the default reverts to matching stock Chrome. The
-  // app-menu toggle (toggleConfirmToQuit:) and the new Settings toggle both
-  // read/write this SAME pref, so they always agree.
-  registry->RegisterBooleanPref(prefs::kConfirmToQuitEnabled, true);
+  // Atrium: opt-IN, unlike stock Chrome's true. An earlier change had
+  // restored the stock default on the theory that the Settings > General "Ask
+  // before quitting" toggle made it safely opt-out-able. It does not, because
+  // of where the gate sits: Cmd+Q resolves to IDC_EXIT (a RESERVED command,
+  // matched off the app menu's terminate: item), so
+  // ChromeCommandDispatcherDelegate::prePerformKeyEquivalent claims the chord
+  // and runs chrome::AttemptUserExit -> BrowserProcessPlatformPart::
+  // AttemptExit -> confirmQuitIfNeeded. With the pref on, a plain TAP fails
+  // the hold-to-quit loop and the quit is cancelled - so Cmd+Q reads as
+  // "randomly does nothing" while the app-menu item still quits (that route
+  // has no key event, so the gate never engages). Defaulting to false makes
+  // the tap quit; the toggle still turns the hold-to-quit guard on for anyone
+  // who wants it. The app-menu toggle (toggleConfirmToQuit:) and the Settings
+  // toggle read/write this SAME pref, so all three always agree.
+  registry->RegisterBooleanPref(prefs::kConfirmToQuitEnabled, false);
 }
 
 }  // namespace confirm_quit
